\title{Graph Linear Notation:\\ A Complete Numeric Invariant for Finite Simple Graphs}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-06-20}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We introduce and rigorously establish \emph{graph linear notation} ($\gln$), a single natural-number invariant attached to every finite simple graph on a labeled vertex set $\mathrm{Fin}\,n = \{0,1,\dots,n-1\}$. The construction proceeds in two stages. First, each \emph{labeled} graph is encoded as a natural number $\adjCode(G)$ by reading its adjacency matrix as a base-$2$ integer with one bit per ordered cell. We prove this encoding is injective. Second, we define $\gln(G)$ to be the maximum of $\adjCode$ over all $n!$ relabelings of the vertices. Maximizing over the relabeling action quotients out the arbitrary choice of vertex names. Our central theorem is that $\gln$ is a \textbf{complete invariant} for graph isomorphism: $\gln(G) = \gln(H)$ if and only if $G$ and $H$ are isomorphic. We give the full definitions, the statements of all supporting lemmas, and detailed proof sketches, and we discuss the relationship of $\gln$ to canonical labeling, the enumeration of isomorphism classes, and probabilistic behavior under the Erd\H{o}s--R\'enyi model. All results have been formally verified in the Lean 4 proof assistant; this paper presents the mathematics those formal proofs certify.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} graph isomorphism, complete invariant, canonical form, adjacency matrix, binary encoding, permutation action, finite simple graph.

\section{Introduction}

A \emph{finite simple graph} is a finite set of vertices together with a symmetric, irreflexive adjacency relation. Two graphs are \textbf{isomorphic} when one can be transformed into the other by renaming vertices. The \emph{graph isomorphism problem} --- deciding whether two given graphs are isomorphic --- is a celebrated computational problem whose precise complexity remains open; it is in $\mathsf{NP}$, is not known to be $\mathsf{NP}$-complete, and is solvable in quasipolynomial time by Babai's algorithm.

A powerful conceptual tool for reasoning about isomorphism is a \textbf{complete invariant}: a function $I$ from graphs to some value domain such that $I(G) = I(H)$ exactly when $G \cong H$. A complete invariant converts the relational question ``are these isomorphic?'' into the equality question ``are these values equal?''. The strongest form of a complete invariant is a \textbf{canonical form}: a rule selecting, from each isomorphism class, one distinguished representative.

This paper develops a complete \emph{numeric} invariant valued in the natural numbers, obtained by the time-honored strategy of \emph{canonicalization by optimization}. We encode each labeled graph as an integer and then take the maximum encoding over all relabelings. The maximizing relabeling yields a canonical adjacency matrix; the maximal integer is the canonical name. We call the resulting integer the \textbf{graph linear notation} of the graph.

The contribution is twofold. Mathematically, we give clean, self-contained proofs that this familiar recipe is correct in both directions (soundness and completeness). Foundationally, every statement below has been machine-checked in Lean 4 with Mathlib, so the invariant is certified, not merely argued.

\section{Definitions}

Throughout, fix $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and work with simple graphs on the vertex type $\mathrm{Fin}\,n = \{0,1,\dots,n-1\}$. We write $G.\Adj\,i\,j$ for the proposition that vertices $i$ and $j$ are adjacent in $G$; adjacency is symmetric and irreflexive.

\subsection{Relabeling (the permutation action)}

\begin{definition}[Permuted graph]
For a permutation $\sigma \in \Sym(\mathrm{Fin}\,n)$ and a graph $G$, define the \emph{relabeled graph} $\permuteGraph(\sigma, G)$ by
\[
\big(\permuteGraph(\sigma, G)\big).\Adj\,i\,j \iff G.\Adj\,(\sigma i)\,(\sigma j).
\]
Formally this is the comap of $G$ along $\sigma$.
\end{definition}

Two basic identities hold: $\permuteGraph(1, G) = G$ (\texttt{permuteGraph\_one}) and $\permuteGraph(\tau, \permuteGraph(\sigma, G)) = \permuteGraph(\sigma\tau, G)$ (\texttt{permuteGraph\_comp}).

\subsection{Isomorphism}

\begin{definition}[Explicit graph isomorphism]
Graphs $G$ and $H$ are \emph{isomorphic}, written $\IsGraphIso(G,H)$, if there is a permutation $\sigma$ with
\[
\forall\, i\,j,\quad G.\Adj\,i\,j \iff H.\Adj\,(\sigma i)\,(\sigma j).
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Isomorphism as relabeling]\label{lem:iso-relabel}
$\IsGraphIso(G,H)$ holds if and only if $G = \permuteGraph(\sigma, H)$ for some $\sigma$ (\texttt{isGraphIso\_iff\_eq\_permute}).
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Unfolding the definition, the condition $G.\Adj\,i\,j \Leftrightarrow H.\Adj\,(\sigma i)(\sigma j)$ for all $i,j$ is exactly the statement that the adjacency relations of $G$ and $\permuteGraph(\sigma, H)$ coincide, i.e.\ that the two graphs are equal by extensionality.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The adjacency code}

\begin{definition}[Adjacency bit]
For a graph $G$ and vertices $i, j$, set $\adjBit(G, i, j) = 1$ if $G.\Adj\,i\,j$ and $0$ otherwise.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Adjacency code]
The \emph{adjacency code} of $G$ is
\[
\adjCode(G) = \sum_{i \in \mathrm{Fin}\,n}\ \sum_{j \in \mathrm{Fin}\,n}\ \adjBit(G, i, j)\cdot 2^{\,i\cdot n + j}.
\]
Equivalently, reading the $n\times n$ adjacency matrix in row-major order produces an $n^2$-bit binary numeral whose value is $\adjCode(G)$. The exponent function $(i,j)\mapsto i\cdot n + j$ assigns each ordered cell a distinct bit position.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Graph linear notation}

The set of codes realized by all relabelings of $G$,
\[
\Orbit(G) = \{\, \adjCode(\permuteGraph(\sigma, G)) : \sigma \in \Sym(\mathrm{Fin}\,n) \,\},
\]
is a finite, nonempty subset of $\mathbb{N}$ (\texttt{orbitCodes\_nonempty}).

\begin{definition}[Graph linear notation]
The \emph{graph linear notation} of $G$ is
\[
\gln(G) = \max\ \Orbit(G) = \max_{\sigma \in \Sym(\mathrm{Fin}\,n)}\ \adjCode(\permuteGraph(\sigma, G)).
\]
\end{definition}

\section{The encoding is injective}

\begin{lemma}[Cell positions are distinct]\label{lem:pair}
The map $(i,j)\mapsto i\cdot n + j$ from $\mathrm{Fin}\,n \times \mathrm{Fin}\,n$ to $\mathbb{N}$ is injective (\texttt{pairEncode\_injective}).
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Suppose $a\cdot n + b = c\cdot n + d$ with $b, d < n$. Reducing modulo $n$ gives $b = d$ (since $(an + b)\bmod n = b$ when $b<n$). Cancelling the equal remainders leaves $a\cdot n = c\cdot n$; as $n>0$ (it bounds $b$), we cancel to get $a = c$. Hence $(a,b)=(c,d)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Bit-coefficient uniqueness]\label{lem:bit}
Let $\iota$ be a finite index type, $e:\iota\to\mathbb{N}$ an injective exponent function, and $f, g : \iota \to \mathbb{N}$ coefficient functions with $f(i), g(i) \le 1$ for all $i$. If $\sum_{i} f(i)\,2^{e(i)} = \sum_{i} g(i)\,2^{e(i)}$, then $f = g$ (\texttt{bit\_coeff\_injective}).
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
This is uniqueness of binary representation. Suppose for contradiction that $f$ and $g$ differ, and let $A = \{ i : f(i) \ne g(i)\}$ be nonempty. Form the integer identity $\sum_{i\in A}(f(i)-g(i))\,2^{e(i)} = 0$ (terms outside $A$ cancel). Let $m \in A$ minimize $e$ over $A$. Factor out $2^{e(m)} \ne 0$ to obtain $\sum_{i\in A}(f(i)-g(i))\,2^{\,e(i)-e(m)} = 0$. Reduce modulo $2$: every term with $i \ne m$ has $e(i) - e(m) \ge 1$ (injectivity of $e$ gives the strict inequality), so those terms vanish mod $2$, leaving $(f(m) - g(m)) \equiv 0 \pmod 2$. But $f(m), g(m) \in \{0,1\}$ with $f(m)\ne g(m)$ forces $f(m)-g(m) = \pm 1$, which is odd --- a contradiction. Hence $A = \varnothing$ and $f = g$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Adjacency code is injective]\label{thm:code-inj}
The map $\adjCode : \mathrm{SimpleGraph}(\mathrm{Fin}\,n) \to \mathbb{N}$ is injective (\texttt{adjCode\_injective}).
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Suppose $\adjCode(G) = \adjCode(H)$. Re-index the double sum as a single sum over pairs $p = (i,j)$, with exponent $e(p) = p_1\cdot n + p_2$ (injective by Lemma~\ref{lem:pair}) and coefficients $f(p) = \adjBit(G, p_1, p_2)$, $g(p) = \adjBit(H, p_1, p_2)$, each $\le 1$. The hypothesis says $\sum_p f(p)2^{e(p)} = \sum_p g(p)2^{e(p)}$, so Lemma~\ref{lem:bit} yields $f = g$, i.e.\ $\adjBit(G,i,j) = \adjBit(H,i,j)$ for all $i,j$. Since the bit equals $1$ exactly when its vertices are adjacent, $G.\Adj\,i\,j \Leftrightarrow H.\Adj\,i\,j$ for all $i,j$, and by extensionality $G = H$.
\end{proof}

\section{Graph linear notation is a complete invariant}

\begin{theorem}[Attainment]\label{thm:attain}
For every $G$ there exists $\sigma$ with $\gln(G) = \adjCode(\permuteGraph(\sigma, G))$ (\texttt{gln\_attained}).
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\gln(G)$ is the maximum of the finite nonempty set $\Orbit(G)$, hence is an element of that set. Any element of $\Orbit(G)$ equals $\adjCode(\permuteGraph(\sigma, G))$ for some witnessing $\sigma$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Isomorphism invariance]\label{thm:inv}
If $\IsGraphIso(G,H)$ then $\gln(G) = \gln(H)$ (\texttt{gln\_iso\_invariant}).
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Lemma~\ref{lem:iso-relabel}, $G = \permuteGraph(\rho, H)$ for some $\rho$. Using the composition law, $\permuteGraph(\sigma, G) = \permuteGraph(\rho\sigma, H)$ for all $\sigma$. As $\sigma$ ranges over all permutations, so does $\rho\sigma$ (left multiplication is a bijection of the symmetric group). Therefore $\Orbit(G) = \Orbit(H)$, and equal finite sets have equal maxima.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Completeness]\label{thm:complete}
If $\gln(G) = \gln(H)$ then $\IsGraphIso(G,H)$ (\texttt{gln\_complete}).
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Theorem~\ref{thm:attain}, choose $\sigma, \tau$ attaining the maxima: $\gln(G) = \adjCode(\permuteGraph(\sigma, G))$ and $\gln(H) = \adjCode(\permuteGraph(\tau, H))$. The hypothesis equates the right-hand sides, so by injectivity (Theorem~\ref{thm:code-inj}), $\permuteGraph(\sigma, G) = \permuteGraph(\tau, H)$. Applying $\permuteGraph(\sigma^{-1}, -)$ to both sides and simplifying with the composition and identity laws gives $G = \permuteGraph(\tau\sigma^{-1}, H)$, so by Lemma~\ref{lem:iso-relabel}, $\IsGraphIso(G,H)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Complete invariant]\label{thm:main}
For all graphs $G, H$ on $\mathrm{Fin}\,n$,
\[
\gln(G) = \gln(H) \iff \IsGraphIso(G,H). \qquad (\texttt{gln\_eq\_iff\_iso})
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The forward direction is Theorem~\ref{thm:complete}; the reverse is Theorem~\ref{thm:inv}.
\end{proof}

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Computing the adjacency code.} Given the adjacency matrix, the code follows directly: read $n^2$ cells and accumulate powers of two, costing $O(n^2)$ bit operations on an integer of $O(n^2)$ bits.

\paragraph{Computing graph linear notation (brute force).} Enumerate all $n!$ permutations, relabel, code, and take the maximum, realizing the canonical labeling. The complexity is $O(n! \cdot n^2)$ integer operations --- a definitionally faithful reference rather than a practical canonizer.

\paragraph{Isomorphism testing via notation.} Theorem~\ref{thm:main} reduces isomorphism testing to integer comparison: $G \cong H \iff \gln(G) = \gln(H)$. While not asymptotically faster than direct search, it cleanly separates the invariant (a number) from the decision (equality).

\section{Applications and consequences}

\textbf{Counting isomorphism classes.} Because $\gln$ is a complete invariant, the number of distinct values it takes on $\mathrm{SimpleGraph}(\mathrm{Fin}\,n)$ equals the number of isomorphism classes of graphs on $n$ vertices: $1,1,2,4,11,34,156,\dots$ (sequence A000088). Each fiber of $\gln$ is exactly one isomorphism class.

\textbf{Canonical labeling.} The attaining permutation of Theorem~\ref{thm:attain} is a canonical labeling; the relabeled graph is a canonical representative, underlying practical deduplication of molecular graphs, program call graphs, and combinatorial structures.

\textbf{Dictionary order.} Since the highest set bit dominates the integer order, $\gln$ realizes a lexicographic maximization of the adjacency bit-string, connecting the invariant to standard canonical-form definitions.

\section{Discussion and limitations}

Graph linear notation provides a mathematically airtight \emph{definition} of a canonical name and a \emph{certified} proof of completeness. It does not accelerate isomorphism testing: the reference computation is exponential in $n$. The value is foundational --- it pins down what a numeric canonical invariant should satisfy and verifies that the maximize-over-relabelings recipe meets the specification in both directions. Encoding both off-diagonal cells is harmless (symmetry makes the two bits agree), and working over $\mathrm{Fin}\,n$ with the symmetric-group action makes ``isomorphism = relabeling'' a clean, formalizable statement.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[Image of $\gln$ enumerates A000088]
For every $N$, the number of distinct values of $\gln$ over $\mathrm{SimpleGraph}(\mathrm{Fin}\,N)$ equals $A000088(N)$: $1, 1, 2, 4, 11, 34, 156, \dots$. Completeness turns the fiber structure of $\gln$ into a bijection between its image and the set of isomorphism classes, so counting notations \emph{is} counting graphs. The remaining step is a finite, decidable cardinality computation.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Canonical labelings exist but are not unique]
The maximizing \emph{graph} is unique, yet the set of maximizing \emph{permutations} has cardinality exactly $|\Aut(G)|$ for every $G$. The maximizing permutations form a coset of the automorphism group: if $\sigma, \tau$ both maximize, then $\sigma\tau^{-1}$ is an automorphism. This is an orbit--stabilizer count.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Monotonicity only up to relabeling]
Adding an edge to $G$ can \emph{decrease} $\gln$ (the maximizing ordering may change), but the per-ordering code at a fixed labeling is monotone. The separation between per-ordering code (monotone) and maximized $\gln$ (not monotone) can be probed with explicit $4$-vertex examples.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Probabilistic concentration]
For the Erd\H{o}s--R\'enyi model $G(n,1/2)$, the normalized notation $\log_2(\gln(G))/n^2$ concentrates around $1/2$ with fluctuations $o(1)$ in probability, reflecting that roughly half of the $n^2$ ordered cells are set in the maximizing ordering. Since the code is a sum of $2^{\mathrm{idx}}$ over set cells, its logarithm is governed by the highest set bit --- a max-type extremal quantity.
\end{conjecture}

\section{Conclusion}

We defined graph linear notation $\gln(G)$ as the maximum binary adjacency code over all vertex relabelings, and established that it is a complete invariant for graph isomorphism: $\gln(G) = \gln(H) \iff G \cong H$. The proof rests on distinct bit positions (Lemma~\ref{lem:pair}), uniqueness of binary expansions (Lemma~\ref{lem:bit}), injectivity of the code (Theorem~\ref{thm:code-inj}), attainment of the maximum (Theorem~\ref{thm:attain}), and the symmetric-group orbit argument (Theorems~\ref{thm:inv}--\ref{thm:complete}). The construction is a clean, fully verified instance of canonicalization by optimization.

